Instead of calculating a height while returning from recursion, this helper carries the current level down the tree. When it reaches a null child, the best level found so far is kept.

For a root at level `1`, every move to the left or right adds one. This also makes the one-child case simple because the existing child is still visited.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
private:
    void visit(TreeNode* node, int level, int& answer) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return;
        }

        if (level > answer) {
            answer = level;
        }

        visit(node->left, level + 1, answer);
        visit(node->right, level + 1, answer);
    }

public:
    int maxDepth(TreeNode* root) {
        int answer = 0;
        visit(root, 1, answer);
        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because both branches are checked for every node.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the call stack. The input tree is not modified.

A missing node contributes depth `0`. For every real node, the longer child path is chosen and the current node adds one more level.

The empty tree case is handled first, so a tree with just one node returns `1`.

```go
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * type TreeNode struct {
 *     Val int
 *     Left *TreeNode
 *     Right *TreeNode
 * }
 */

func maxDepth(root *TreeNode) int {
	if root == nil {
		return 0
	}

	leftDepth := maxDepth(root.Left)
	rightDepth := maxDepth(root.Right)

	if leftDepth > rightDepth {
		return leftDepth + 1
	}
	return rightDepth + 1
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, because every node is visited once.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the recursive calls, where `h` is the tree height. A one-sided tree can use `O(n)` stack space.

The queue keeps all nodes from the next level. At the start of each loop, its current size tells how many nodes belong to the level being processed. After removing those nodes, the depth increases by one.

A null root returns `0` without creating a queue. For example, with levels `[1]`, `[2, 3]`, and `[4]`, the counter becomes `3`.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Queue;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int data) { data = data; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public int maxDepth(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer(root);
        int depth = 0;

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int nodesInLevel = queue.size();

            for (int i = 0; i < nodesInLevel; i++) {
                TreeNode current = queue.poll();

                if (current.left != null) {
                    queue.offer(current.left);
                }
                if (current.right != null) {
                    queue.offer(current.right);
                }
            }

            depth++;
        }

        return depth;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(w)`, where `w` is the largest number of nodes in one level. In the worst case, this is `O(n)`.

This version avoids recursion and an extra stack. Morris traversal temporarily links the rightmost node of a left subtree back to its parent. The link is removed later, so the input tree is restored before the method returns.

The current depth is adjusted when a temporary link is created or removed. This is less common than recursion, but it keeps extra working space constant.

```csharp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     public int val;
 *     public TreeNode left;
 *     public TreeNode right;
 *     public TreeNode(int val=0, TreeNode left=null, TreeNode right=null) {
 *         this.val = val;
 *         this.left = left;
 *         this.right = right;
 *     }
 * }
 */

public class Solution {
    public int maxDepth(TreeNode root) {
        int depth = 0;
        int answer = 0;
        TreeNode current = root;

        while (current != null) {
            if (current.left == null) {
                depth++;
                if (depth > answer) {
                    answer = depth;
                }
                current = current.right;
                continue;
            }

            TreeNode predecessor = current.left;
            int steps = 1;

            while (predecessor.right != null && predecessor.right != current) {
                predecessor = predecessor.right;
                steps++;
            }

            if (predecessor.right == null) {
                depth++;
                if (depth > answer) {
                    answer = depth;
                }
                predecessor.right = current;
                current = current.left;
            } else {
                predecessor.right = null;
                depth -= steps;
                current = current.right;
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` in the usual Morris traversal analysis.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space. The tree is changed only temporarily and is restored during the walk.

An empty tree returns `0`, and a single-node tree returns `1`.

